 ## Rules of the loop
 
 - **Red before green.** Write the failing test first, then only enough code to pass it. Don't anticipate future tests or add speculative features.
-- **One slice at a time.** One seam, one test, one minimal implementation per cycle.
+- **Stubs before tests.** Stub the new names first (empty functions and methods), so a red test fails on its assertions, not on an import error. A test the user asks for on code that already exists can't fail first: break that code on purpose, watch the test fail, then restore it. `mutate-check` (`-h`) does all three.
+- **One slice at a time.** One seam, its test or a few tests (every one red first), one minimal implementation per cycle.
 - **Refactoring is not part of the loop.** It belongs to the review stage (see the `code-review` skill), not the red → green implementation cycle.
